# Git commits on Windows (PowerShell)

The agent shell here is **PowerShell**, not bash. Bash heredocs fail with:

```text
Missing file specification after redirection operator.
```

**Do not** use this (from the generic commit user-rule examples):

```bash
git commit -m "$(cat <<'EOF'
Message here.

EOF
)"
```

There is nothing to “install” to make that work inside PowerShell. Use one of the forms below instead.

## Preferred: two `-m` flags (subject + body)

```powershell
git commit -m "Subject line in imperative mood." -m "Optional second paragraph with more context. Why, not what."
```

Git joins multiple `-m` arguments with a blank line — that is the usual subject/body split.

## Multi-line body via a PowerShell here-string

Only when the body needs real newlines inside one paragraph block:

```powershell
$msg = @'
Subject line in imperative mood.

Body with real newlines if needed.
Second body line.
'@
git commit -m $msg
```

Use single-quoted `@'...'@` so `$` and `` ` `` in the message are not expanded.

## Push

After a successful commit, when the user asked to push:

```powershell
git push -u origin HEAD
git status
```

Still follow `commit-everything.mdc` (stage the full related working tree) and the usual git safety rules (no force-push to main, no amend unless allowed, no secrets).
